          <p>Germany hosts several of Europe's finest certified dark sky parks. The Eifel National Park, Germany's first IDA-certified dark sky park, offers Maar volcanic crater lakes for stunning reflection photography just 70 km from Cologne. Westhavelland near Berlin is one of Europe's largest dark sky parks at 150,000 hectares, with flat Brandenburg horizons ideal for aurora observation. The Rhön Biosphere Reserve's "Starland Rhön" programme adds a high-plateau dimension, while the Bavarian Forest forms a cross-border dark sky zone with the Czech Republic's Šumava National Park.</p>
